window.__labLoaded({"id":"8.1","title":"Correlation: Do Two Numbers Move Together?","goal":"Measure how strongly study hours and exam scores move together for the lesson's eight students, with Pearson's r, Spearman's rho and a scatter plot.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the data and look at it","text":"Eight students: hours studied and exam score. Always draw the scatter plot before trusting a correlation number, because the plot shows the pattern, outliers and curves that one number hides.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nhours = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8])\nscore = np.array([48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82])\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.scatter(hours, score, color='#1f77b4')\nplt.xlabel('Hours studied')\nplt.ylabel('Exam score')\nplt.title('Study hours and exam score')\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Pearson's r","text":"pearsonr measures the strength of a straight-line link from -1 to +1. It also returns a p-value for the test that the true correlation is 0, which uses the t distribution with n - 2 degrees of freedom.","code":"res = stats.pearsonr(hours, score)\n\nprint(f'r       = {res.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'r squared = {res.statistic ** 2:.3f}')\nprint(f'p-value = {res.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint('df      =', len(hours) - 2)","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Spearman's rho (the rank version)","text":"Spearman replaces each value by its rank and then correlates the ranks. It catches any steadily rising or falling pattern, curved or not, and is less bothered by outliers.","code":"sp = stats.spearmanr(hours, score)\n\nprint(f'rho     = {sp.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'p-value = {sp.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Correlation is not causation","text":"A strong r tells us hours and score rise together. It cannot tell us that studying causes the higher scores: keen, well-prepared students may both study more and score higher. Only a well-designed experiment can settle cause.","code":"print(f'r = {res.statistic:.2f}: the two variables rise together.')\nprint('That does NOT prove that extra hours caused extra points.')\nprint('Something else (motivation, prior skill) could drive both.')","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"hours <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)\nscore <- c(48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82)\nplot(hours, score)\ncor.test(hours, score)\ncor.test(hours, score, method = \"spearman\")","expect":"t = 5.2757, df = 6, p-value = 0.001872; 95 percent CI 0.5 to 0.98; cor 0.9066; Spearman S = 12, p-value = 0.02181, rho 0.857","note":"cor.test gives Pearson's r by default and also a confidence interval. For Spearman with 8 values R reports an exact p-value, which can differ a little from the approximate p-value in Python."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Correlate > Bivariate (tick Pearson and Spearman); Graphs > Chart Builder > Scatter/Dot","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / hours score.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 48  2 62  3 55  4 66  5 63  6 74  7 70  8 82\nEND DATA.\nGRAPH /SCATTERPLOT(BIVAR)=hours WITH score.\nCORRELATIONS /VARIABLES=hours score.\nNONPAR CORR /VARIABLES=hours score /PRINT=SPEARMAN TWOTAIL.","note":"CORRELATIONS gives Pearson's r with its two-tailed Sig.; NONPAR CORR with SPEARMAN gives rho. SPSS uses an approximate p-value for Spearman."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 8\ngenerate hours = _n\ngenerate score = real(word(\"48 62 55 66 63 74 70 82\", _n))\nscatter score hours\npwcorr hours score, sig\nspearman hours score","note":"pwcorr prints Pearson's r with its p-value (sig); spearman prints rho and its p-value. scatter draws the plot."},"try":["Change the last score from 82 to 40 and predict what happens to r. Does Spearman's rho move as much?","Put the scores in reverse order (82 first). Predict the sign of r, then check."]});
